    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-129332",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d5666035-199d-56aa-a6f4-f65a3f847b42",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring",
      "problem": "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{xz+yz}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{xz+yz}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{(-1)(2)+1(2)}{(-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2+2}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{xz+yz}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z(x+y)}{(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z\\cancel{(x+y)}}{\\cancel{(x+y)}(x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z}{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z}{x-y} & =\\frac{2}{-1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-1.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "-4"
            },
