      "math_academy_id": "q-129348",
      "knowledge_point_id": "7d53554f-6d77-56f9-8950-d8e31a7f4fdb",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions Using Conjugate Multiplication",
      "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}-3}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}=$",
      "worked_solution": "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}-3}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{({0}^{2})({0}^{2})+9}-3}{({0}^{2})({0}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}-3}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}-3)(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9-9}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{({0}^{2})({0}^{2})+9}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{6}.$",
