    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-129346",
      "knowledge_point_id": "7d53554f-6d77-56f9-8950-d8e31a7f4fdb",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions Using Conjugate Multiplication",
      "problem": "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{x+y-{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+y}-z}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{x+y-{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+y}-z} & =\\frac{1+0-{1}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+0}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+0-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{x+y-{z}^{2}}{\\sqrt{x+y}-z} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{(x+y-{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)}{(\\sqrt{x+y}-z)(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{(x+y-{z}^{2})(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)}{x+y-{z}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}\\frac{\\cancel{(x+y-{z}^{2})}(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)}{\\cancel{x+y-{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}(\\sqrt{x+y}+z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,0,1)}(\\sqrt{x+y}+z) & =\\sqrt{1+0}+1 \\\\  & =1+0+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$",
