    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-74901",
      "knowledge_point_id": "7d53554f-6d77-56f9-8950-d8e31a7f4fdb",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions Using Conjugate Multiplication",
      "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{1+xy}-1}=$",
      "worked_solution": "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{1+xy}-1}=\\frac{{0}^{2}-0}{\\sqrt{1+0\\cdot 1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{1+xy}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{(\\sqrt{1+xy}-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{x(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{(\\sqrt{1+xy})^{2}-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{x(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{xy} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,1)}\\frac{(x-1)(\\sqrt{1+xy}+1)}{y} & =\\frac{(0-1)(\\sqrt{1+0\\cdot 1}+1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{1} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$",
