    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-74958",
      "knowledge_point_id": "7d53554f-6d77-56f9-8950-d8e31a7f4fdb",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions Using Conjugate Multiplication",
      "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}}=$",
      "worked_solution": "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{1}-1}{1-{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-y)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+y} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$",
