    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-129408",
      "knowledge_point_id": "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $4$ along the path $y={x}^{4}$\n- The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $x=0$\n- The limit does not exist",
      "worked_solution": "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2({0}^{2})\\sqrt{0}}{{0}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{4}}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}}}{{x}^{4}+({x}^{4})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{8}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}(1+{x}^{4})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2\\cancel{{x}^{4}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{4}}(1+{x}^{4})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+{0}^{4}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,x=0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}\\frac{2({0}^{2})\\sqrt{y}}{{0}^{4}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{y\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$",
