      "math_academy_id": "e-3330",
      "knowledge_point_id": "1f2ac99b-55b2-5742-81ab-d2961db1250f",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions at Points of Continuity",
      "problem": "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,2)}} \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)= \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,2)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-1,2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,2)}\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{x+y} & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot {2}^{3}}{(-1)+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{1} \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3331",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d5666035-199d-56aa-a6f4-f65a3f847b42",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring",
      "problem": "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac 0 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2} = \\dfrac{(-1)^2(1) + (-1)(1)^2}{(-1)^2 - (1)^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy(x+y)}{(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy}{(x-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy}{(x-y)} & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 1}{-1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\dfrac{1}{2}.$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-6239",
      "knowledge_point_id": "7d53554f-6d77-56f9-8950-d8e31a7f4fdb",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions Using Conjugate Multiplication",
      "problem": "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2-4y}{x-2\\sqrt{y}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{2^2-4 \\cdot 1}{2-2\\sqrt{1}} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4y}{x-2\\sqrt{y}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4y)(x+2\\sqrt{y})}{(x-2\\sqrt{y})(x+2\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4y)(x+2\\sqrt{y})}{{x}^{2}-(2\\sqrt{y})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4y)(x+2\\sqrt{y})}{{x}^{2}-4y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}(x+2\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}(x+2\\sqrt{y}) & =2+2\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $4.$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3366",
      "knowledge_point_id": "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions",
      "problem": "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Direct substitution gives an indeterminate form, but the function cannot be simplified by canceling a common factor in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\dfrac{0 \\cdot (0 + 2 \\cdot 0)}{0^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x+2x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x+2(-x))}{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\text{DNE}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    }
